<template>
    <children>
        <li id="37.lp5"/>
        <li id="36.lp5"/>
        <li id="28.lp5"/>
        <li id="45.lp5"/>
    </children>
    <heading>Operations</heading>
    <keywords>
        <li>operations</li>
        <li>generator</li>
        <li>coroutine</li>
        <li>command handler</li>
        <li>initialization</li>
        <li>modal dialog</li>
    </keywords>
    <section data-lp5-kind="explanation">
        <p>Operations express command legality and the continuation of an interaction. The state-machine source keeps that control flow together; nested <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Iterators_and_generators">JavaScript generators</a> preserve local variables and the work following a child call. Async functions perform the waiting and report completion through commands, leaving the state handler available to interpret incoming commands.</p>
        <p>The controller and generated states share this protocol:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><code>yield self()</code> consumes the command and waits for the next one in the same handler.</li>
            <li><code>yield child</code> calls a fresh, primed child with the same command. The caller remains suspended until the child finishes.</li>
            <li><code>return</code> resumes the caller with the child's completion command. The returned JavaScript value is ignored; without a caller, dispatch ends.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Compilation belongs to <code>startup()</code> so syntax failures use the same <a href="#25.lp5">fatal-error reporting</a> as runtime failures. Defining the unit earlier must not start command handling. The <a href="#36.lp5">compiler</a> and machine stay private because callers need to send commands, not manipulate state factories.</p>
        <p>The <a href="#37.lp5">command state machine</a> and <a href="#28.lp5">async selection work</a> are explained in their own articles. The production controller currently supports calls, waits, and returns; the compiler's replacing transitions remain experimental and are exercised only by separate test drivers. The <a href="#45.lp5">article save helper</a> reports save success or failure as commands for the active edit operation.</p>
    </section>
    <section data-lp5-kind="code">
        <name>JavaScript units</name>
        <code><![CDATA[
let operations = (() => {
    ]]><lp5- ref="State Machine Compiler"/><![CDATA[

    ]]><lp5- ref="SelectProject async work"/><![CDATA[

    ]]><lp5- ref="Article editing async work"/><![CDATA[

    ]]><lp5- ref="Command state machine"/><![CDATA[

    let stateMachine;
    function startup() {
        const code = compileStateMachine(stateMachineSource);
        const define = util.scriptToFunction(code, "command-state-machine", "view", "asyncSelectProject", "asyncReloadProject", "asyncSaveArticle");
        stateMachine = define(view, asyncSelectProject, asyncReloadProject, asyncSaveArticle);
        controller.init(stateMachine.states[stateMachine.initialState]());
    }

    return { startup };
})();
]]></code>
    </section>
</template>
